The _only_ benefit to advancing Healing skill is reduced fail rates [1].  
The formulas are somewhat complicated (spl1-343.txt 352-410), but the 
short version is that for Healers, the benefit is likely to be 
nonexistent, or very small:

The key spells in the school are Healing and Extra Healing.  Cure 
Sickness is not relevant in until the late-game, and then only as 
insurance.  A quick examination of the "difficulty" formula (394-400) 
reveals that for characters at XL3 and above, improving spell skill will 
NEVER affect fail rates for level one spells. [2] So much for Healing.

Now, Healers start at Basic in the school, so the "difficulty" of Extra 
Healing is initially 1, falls to 0 at XL3, -1 at XL6, etc.  Each negative 
point of "difficulty" improves the fail rate of Extra Healing by 
approximately 5%.  But because of the cap (399), Extra Healing does not 
benefit from a "difficulty" of less than -4.

 Raising the spell school to Skilled subtracts 6 from "difficulty".  At 
XL6, this is a 15% difference.  At XL9, the difference is only 10%.  At 
XL12, only 5%.  But if you stay away from metal armor, your fail rate at 
Extra Healing is going to be 0%, without having to boost spell skill. 




[1] Technically, restore ability spell also improves.

[2] For level two spells, there is never a benefit to raising spell skill 
above Basic, and at XL18, Unskilled is just as good as Basic.


